CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member erase\(iterator->const_iterator\) in template body: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*found no match for symbol.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
A user-defined conversion (iterator -> const_iterator) via a converting
constructor template whose parameter is a class-template-id (Iter<Q, C>) is
applied during member-call overload resolution inside an instantiated function
template body.  The overloaded member erase(const_iterator, const_iterator) is
viable for iterator arguments, so the call runs and the assertion holds.

Root cause and fix (cpp_typecheck_conversions.cpp,
user_defined_conversion_sequence template-constructor fallback): when no
non-template converting constructor matched, the fallback builds a temporary of
the target type via new_temporary and inspects it for the constructor that
performed the conversion.  The search (`find_ctor`) returned the *first*
constructor anywhere in the result, which for a prvalue argument such as
`c.begin()` was the source iterator's own materialisation constructor (a ctor
of a *different* type), causing the valid conversion to be rejected.  The fix
scopes `find_ctor` to constructors of the *target* type, so the source
argument's materialisation constructor is ignored.  This matches the standard's
[over.match.copy] / [class.conv.ctor]/2 (only the target's non-explicit
converting constructor participates) and made `iterator -> const_iterator` work
for prvalue arguments as it already did for lvalues (`const_iterator c = it;`).
This is the header-free analogue of std::erase_if's
`__cont.erase(iterator, iterator)` (see cpp20_vector_erase_if).
